      "worked": "Exactly one head has sequences $HTT,THT,TTH$; exactly two heads has sequences $HHT,HTH,THH$. Each event has three equally likely arrangements.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{exactly one head})&=3p(1-p)^2\\\\P(\\text{exactly two heads})&=3p^2(1-p)\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe stated relationship gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3p(1-p)^2&=6p^2(1-p)\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $0<p<1$, divide by $3p(1-p)$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-p&=2p\\\\1&=3p\\\\p&=\\frac13\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
